  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "After helping Santa get some exercise, you (Santa's little helper) think it's time the elves get around to actually working on toys.\n\nSanta Claus Lane is home to NNN elves, the iii-th of whom lives XiX_iXi​ meters from the start of the lane.\n\nYou are, once again, tasked to assign elves to work on toys for good little girls and boys.\n\nEach elf must be assigned to work on **exactly** 111 toy, and each toy should have **at least** 222 different elves working on it.\n\nAll elves working on the same toy will meet at the point which minimizes the farthest distance that any of them would need to walk.\n\nFormally, if elves at positions Y1,Y2,…Y_1, Y_2, \\ldotsY1​,Y2​,… are assigned to work on the same toy, they will meet at a point PPP that minimizes max⁡(∣Y1−P∣,∣Y2−P∣,…)\\max(|Y_1 - P|, |Y_2 - P|, \\ldots)max(∣Y1​−P∣,∣Y2​−P∣,…).\n\nNote that PPP need not be an integer.\n\nYou want the elves to start working as quickly as possible. On the other hand, you also don't want to supervise too much work, so it'd be nice if there weren't many toys being worked on.\n\nSo, your task is to find an assignment of elves to toys such that:\n\n\nThe maximum distance that any elf walks is minimized.\nAmong all assignments satisfying the first condition, the number of toys is minimized.\nFind these two values.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a positive integer NNN — the number of elves.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers X1,X2,…,XNX_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_NX1​,X2​,…,XN​, as described in the statement.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line two values: a real number and an integer.\n\nThe first is the minimum possible maximum distance any elf has to walk, and the second is the minimum number of toys needed to achieve such an assignment.\n\n\nThe distance is considered correct if its absolute or relative error doesn't exceed 10−610^{-6}10−6.\n\nFormally, let your output be xxx and the jury's answer be yyy. Your output will be considered correct if ∣x−y∣max⁡(1,y)≤10−6\\frac{|x-y|}{\\max(1, y)} \\leq 10^{-6}max(1,y)∣x−y∣​≤10−6.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "2≤N≤1062 \\leq N \\leq 10^62≤N≤106",
      "1≤X1&lt;X2&lt;…&lt;XN≤1091 \\leq X_1 \\lt X_2 \\lt \\ldots \\lt X_N \\leq 10^91≤X1​&lt;X2​&lt;…&lt;XN​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 10610^6106."
    ],
